  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p3-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "For real numbers $a$ and $b$ with $ab<1$, prove that\n$$\\tan^{-1}(a)+\\tan^{-1}(b)=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{a+b}{1-ab}\\right).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The identity holds for $ab<1$.\n\nApply tangent addition and use the principal range of $\\tan^{-1}$.",
          "worked": "Let $\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(a)$ and $\\beta=\\tan^{-1}(b)$, so $\\alpha,\\beta\\in(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\frac{\\tan\\alpha+\\tan\\beta}{1-\\tan\\alpha\\tan\\beta}\\\\\\tan(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\frac{a+b}{1-ab}.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta-\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta\\\\\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)&=\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta(1-ab)>0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\alpha+\\beta\\in(-\\pi,\\pi)$, this gives $\\alpha+\\beta\\in(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan^{-1}(a)+\\tan^{-1}(b)&=\\alpha+\\beta\\\\\\tan^{-1}(a)+\\tan^{-1}(b)&=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{a+b}{1-ab}\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $b$ in terms of $a$ given that\n$$\\tan^{-1}(a)+\\tan^{-1}(b)=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$b=\\dfrac{1-a}{a+1}$, with $a>-1$.\n\nUse tangent addition and rearrange; the principal inverse-tangent ranges require $a>-1$.",
          "worked": "Let $\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(a)$ and $\\beta=\\tan^{-1}(b)$. Taking tangents and using the addition formula gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{a+b}{1-ab}&=\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\\\\\frac{a+b}{1-ab}&=1\\\\a+b&=1-ab\\\\b(1+a)&=1-a\\\\b&=\\frac{1-a}{1+a}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\beta=\\frac{\\pi}{4}-\\alpha$ must lie in $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$ and $\\alpha\\in(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2})$, we need $\\alpha>-\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, or $a>-1$. For these values,\n$$1-ab=\\frac{1+a^2}{1+a}>0,$$\nso the addition identity gives the required principal-angle sum.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 57
      }
    ]
  },
